<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lülin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/17">17</a>–<a href="/years/25">25</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Green Forest</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lülin, or 'Green Forest', were a rebel movement that arose around 17 CE in the hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xin-dynasty">Xin dynasty</a>. In 23 they enthroned a Han kinsman as the Gengshi Emperor, and their armies captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, where Wang Mang was killed. Their regime soon fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows">Red Eyebrows</a>, and the name Lülin became a lasting Chinese term for outlaws.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lulin.jpg" alt="Lülin" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xin_Dynasty_Uprisings.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 17 CE Jing Province was suffering a famine made far worse by the corruption and incompetence of Xin officials, and the starving fought one another over wild plants. Two men of Xinshi, Wang Kuang and Wang Feng, who had settled some of these quarrels, led local peasants to the Lulin hills, from which the movement took its name. Joined by Ma Wu, Wang Chang and Cheng Dan, they raided villages and estates far from the cities for food, growing from 7,000 or 8,000 men to tens of thousands. They hoped to go home to farm when the famine ended and did not dare attack cities.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> listened to courtiers who called them simple criminals and chose force over pardon. In 21 CE the rebels routed 20,000 government troops at Yundu, but in 22 CE an epidemic killed at least 25,000 of them, and they broke into the Xiajiang Force, which moved west, and the Xinshi Force, which moved north, while the Pinglin Force rose nearby.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Liu Yan, of a distant branch of the Han imperial clan, joined the Xinshi and Pinglin Forces and brought in the Xiajiang leaders, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-new-year">Chinese New Year</a> of 23 CE the coalition crushed and killed the governor of Nanyang, Zhen Fu. Wary of Liu Yan's strict discipline, the Xinshi and Pinglin leaders backed his third cousin Liu Xuan instead. After Liu Yan's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Liu Xiu</a> shattered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>'s army at Kunyang, risings broke out across the empire, and the new emperor soon had Liu Yan executed. He moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and in 24 CE to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The emperor and the former Lulin leaders who filled his government ruled so poorly that the provinces fell away. In 25 CE Liu Xiu proclaimed himself emperor, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a>, while plots among the generals divided the court. The emperor executed Chen Mu and Cheng Dan on suspicion, Wang Kuang went over to Zhang Ang, and Zhang and his allies joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows">Red Eyebrows</a>, whose capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> ended the regime; the emperor surrendered in the winter of 25 CE and was killed. Most Lulin leaders faded into obscurity. Historians call the regime the Xuan Han, and treat the Eastern Han as the true restoration of the dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lulin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Lülin</b> peasant rebellion, rebels captured and sacked Chang'an, capital of Wang Mang's short-lived Xin dynasty; Wang Mang was killed two days later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Peasant risings such as the Red Eyebrows and the <b>Lülin</b> spread.</blockquote><a href="/state/xin-dynasty">Xin dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
